  "image_name": "63b94f539ded8a640eebc35bfdb1b91c.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic compares lithium deposition on a hard versus soft substrate and shows how mechanical response affects dendrite formation. In both cases, Li⁺ ions arrive at the surface through an SEI layer and begin to deposit as rounded lithium clusters, creating compressive stress at the interface.\n\nOn the hard substrate, the interface remains relatively fixed, so the growing deposits cannot easily redistribute the stress. The surface becomes rougher, with taller protrusions that concentrate further deposition. As stress builds without relaxation, these peaks develop into dendrite-like structures.\n\nOn the soft substrate, lithium still nucleates and generates compressive stress, but the underlying material appears able to deform laterally and vertically. White arrows indicate stress relaxation or flow along the interface. Because the substrate can accommodate the volume change, the deposited lithium spreads more evenly rather than piling up into sharp features. The final surface is thicker but comparatively smooth and continuous.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that substrate mechanics strongly influence lithium metal growth: rigid support promotes stress accumulation and dendrites, whereas a compliant support relieves stress and enables more uniform deposition.",
